.TH TPAD 8
.SH NAME
aux/tpad \- PS/2 Synaptics touchpad gestures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aux/tpad
[
.B -dp
]
[
.B -D
.I name=value
]
...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tpad
drives a PS/2 Synaptics touchpad from userspace, adding multi-finger
gestures the kernel mouse driver does not provide.
.PP
It writes
.B synaptic
to
.B #m/mousectl
to put the pad into absolute mode, then reads the raw packets from
.B #m/touchpadraw
(see
.IR mouse (3)),
decodes them, and drives the cursor by writing mouse events to
.BR #m/mousein .
It uses the kernel
.B #m
device directly rather than
.B /dev
so that it still reaches the kernel mouse when a window system such as
.IR rio (1)
has mounted its own
.B /dev/mouse
files over it.
While
.I tpad
holds
.B touchpadraw
open the kernel stops decoding the packets itself, so
.I tpad
has sole control of the pad; a muxed trackpoint is passed through as
relative motion.
.PP
The gestures are the same as for a USB precision touchpad; see
.IR nusb (4):
one finger moves the pointer, two fingers scroll, and a physical click is
button 1.
A physical click with two or three fingers, or a light tap of a second or
a second and third finger while one finger rests, is instead the right or
middle menu button; it stays held while a finger remains on the pad, so
sliding to a menu item and lifting selects it.
Basic PS/2 Synaptics reports only one finger's position plus a count, so
the menu gestures work from the finger count while two-finger tracking is
limited.
.PP
The
.B -d
flag traces decoded packets, with their raw bytes, to standard error.
.B -p
prints the current tunables (as
.I name value
pairs) and exits.
.B -D
.I name=value
sets a tunable; it may be repeated.
The knobs are the gesture-engine ones shared with
.IR nusb (4)
.RB ( gainx ,
.BR gainy ,
.BR scroll ,
.BR taptime ,
.BR taptravel ,
.BR suppress ,
.BR scrollstart ,
.BR menutime ,
.BR jump ,
and
.B tapclick
to disable tap-to-click), plus this driver's Synaptics decode
.RB ( xmin ,
.BR xmax ,
.BR ymin ,
.BR ymax
for the coordinate range,
.B zmin
pressure floor, and
.B wpalm
palm-width cutoff).
Since
.I tpad
restarts cheaply, re-run it with new values to tune by feel.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/tpad.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR mouse (3),
.IR mouse (8),
.IR nusb (4)
.SH BUGS
The coordinate range and pressure thresholds are fixed nominal values
rather than queried from the device, and the second finger's position is
not decoded, so only the finger count distinguishes two- from three-finger
gestures.
